# LeakSanitizer suppressions — third-party one-time/opaque allocations ONLY.
#
# Rule for adding a line here: it must name a library we do not build from
# source, and the classification must come from a MEASURED run, not a guess.
# Nothing in the turbo_ocr:: namespace is ever suppressed — a leak with one of
# our frames in the stack is a bug to fix.
#
# Evidence backing the current list (2026-08-11, aarch64 Linux, clean-container
# ASan/RelWithDebInfo build):
#   - full suite, 571/571 passing under ASan: LSan reported 3,744 bytes in 53
#     allocations at exit — every stack routes through FPDF_LoadPage ->
#     CPDF_StreamContentParser::Parse inside the prebuilt libpdfium, with ZERO
#     turbo_ocr frames anywhere in the report.
#   - the same classification holds on macOS by a different instrument:
#     `leaks` reported 26,144 bytes byte-for-byte identical before and after
#     doubling the request count, i.e. one-time init, not per-request.
#
# Evidence for the pthread_cond_broadcast entry (2026-08-11, Arch + RTX 5090,
# CUDA configure under ASAN_OPTIONS=protect_shadow_gap=0, 620/620 passing):
#   - exactly 3 direct leaks of 48 bytes each, every stack being
#     calloc <- pthread_cond_broadcast <- <unknown module> — the anonymous
#     executable pages are the CUDA driver's internal threads, and the calloc
#     is glibc's own lazy condvar bookkeeping, which those threads never tear
#     down before exit. Count is load-independent.
#   - safe to match on that frame: the only allocation inside
#     pthread_cond_broadcast is glibc's internal one — an object of OURS leaked
#     through a condvar would carry its own allocation frame instead.
#
# Usage:
#   LSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=tools/checks/lsan.supp ./build-asan/turbo_ocr_tests
leak:libpdfium
leak:pthread_cond_broadcast
